diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end sweep of the lab found no fault that would explain the helpdesk report. PC11 (172.16.20.11) is presently fully reachable: it pinged both its same-subnet peer pc9 (172.16.20.9, 0% loss) and the cross-subnet host pc14 (172.16.30.14, 0% loss) in the Phase 1 reachability pass. Its interface eth0 is UP, it holds the expected /24 address, its default route points directly to the VLAN20 gateway (172.16.20.254 on mswitch0), and that path is clean. All L2 checks are clean — no duplicate MAC addresses, no switch port DOWN, no static ARP injection. The OSPF control plane is fully converged (9 routers, all Full adjacencies, 18 routes each). No traffic-control shaping, no ACL drops, no resource pressure exist anywhere in the lab. PC11's host_path_snapshot is byte-for-byte identical to those of its VLAN20 neighbours pc9 and pc10, confirming there is no per-host misconfiguration. The zero-resolver (`resolvers=0`) observation is topology-wide baseline, not a pc11-specific anomaly. The dhcp_link_history helper found no suspicious DHCP or carrier-transition events on pc11. In the absence of any mechanism, any historical trace (flap script, syslog entry, carrier counter above startup baseline), or any live measurement showing packet loss or a wrong L2/L3 identity, the helpdesk claim cannot be corroborated. The most probable explanation is that the reported isolation was a transient event (e.g., a brief DHCP lease gap or a momentary switch restart) that resolved itself before investigation began. No corrective action is required.
=== END ===